# plan__ta-anchor-da-sentinel-belongs-on-the-ballot

from: designer (Type-1 owner, `planning/combined-analyzer-optimizer-design.md`)
to: planner (ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh Type-3 owner)
cc: review (PR-2 internal code reviewer) — this answers the open half of your Finding 46/47 pair;
    no action requested of you
date: 2026-08-08
ask: decide — where the `(D-a)` sentinel is written, and whether anything lands in PR-2
re: sibling to `plan__ta-anchor-c11-da-sentinel-unsized.md` and
    `plan__ta-anchor-c11-da-domain-is-exhaustive.md` (both `.WIP`, so not edited). This is a new
    handoff, not an amendment to either.

## Standing

The defect is **mine**. The `FZ-admission` mechanism I froze into the Type 1 at `8c2a9b04`
(a `Reason`-tagged `PRC = 1` sentinel in the anchor's no-variant branch, plus a one-replica target
ceiling at the granting sites) is insufficient as written. Dean asked me to verify whether it would
kill partial scale-from-zero with two variants and TA binding. It would. Every citation below is
`git show HEAD:` against the coder's tip **`2ae440e3`** *"docs: write from-zero admission as deferred
and fix four false premises"* — committed state only, never the working tree. (Line numbers moved
under that commit; an earlier draft of this handoff carried the pre-`2ae440e3` numbers and the wrong
tip label. Substance is unchanged — the code did not move, only its line numbers.)

The coder was right to stop, and right about why — `analyzer_helpers.go:85` states the problem
correctly ("*an anchor-only sentinel makes a variant selectable without making it sizable*"). This
handoff supplies the answer that block routes back to me.

## What I verified

Two variants: `V_run` measured and wanting more replicas, `V_zero` never measured at zero
replicas. TA holds the bind (i.e. saturation failed `Enabled && Live && Informative`).

**Today, with `(D-a)` deferred and nothing writing the tag — safe.** `V_zero`'s anchor PRC is 0,
so `cost_aware_optimizer.go:100` passes it over as unpriceable and `V_run` is picked normally.
Partial scale-from-zero does not happen; nothing else is harmed. There is no live bug to bypass
today.

**With the anchor-only sentinel as the Type 1 specifies — it breaks the whole model's scale-up.**

1. `sortByCostEfficiencyAsc`: `V_zero`'s anchor `Cost` arrives from saturation's (a) identity merge,
   and saturation reports `Cost = 0` for a zero-replica variant (`N5`). `0/1 = 0` ⇒ `V_zero` sorts
   **first**. The bad path is therefore the default ordering, not an unlucky one.
2. `PerReplicaCapacity <= 0` at `:100` — passes. That is the sentinel working as intended.
3. `maxTargetReplicas` returns `(1, true)`; headroom `1 - 0 = 1` ⇒ pick returns `(V_zero, 1)`.
   `allPicked` stays true, so there is no early exit.
4. `roleBottleneckReplicas` (`:624-633`) → `votesFromPickerState` (`analyzer_helpers.go:522-535`)
   reads `prcForVariant(e.Result, v)` — **the ballot entry, not the anchor**. Every entry hits
   `if prc <= 0 { continue }`. Zero votes ⇒ `combineVotes` ⇒ `binder < 0` ⇒ `n = 0`.
5. `roleAggRemaining` (`:698-704`) abstains identically ⇒ `demand = 0` ⇒ `utilByRole = 1.0`, so the
   `deltaUtil <= 0` guard at `:982` does **not** fire.
6. `k` is computed only `if prc > 0 && demand > 0` ⇒ `k = 0` ⇒ `!anyPositive` ⇒ **`break` at
   `:1001`, out of the entire `for anyRoleNeedsScaleUp(...)` loop.** `V_run` is never picked.

So the failure is not "the from-zero variant gets nothing" — it is "**the co-tenant variant loses
its scale-up too**", and per (1) that is the expected ordering.

Note `cost_aware_optimizer.go:103-107` already defends this exact hazard on the *cap* path ("*a
returned 0 makes the caller compute deltaUtil == 0 and break out of the whole model's allocation
loop, so a bounded-out variant would take every variant behind it down with it*"). The sentinel
re-opens the same break through the *demand* path instead.

**Secondary, P/D:** if the never-measured variant is the only one in its role, the other role's
positive `k` keeps `anyPositive` true, so there is no break — instead prefill commits while decode
stays at zero, violating the matched-joint-commit that `allocateForModelPaired` exists to
guarantee. Terminates (it does not spin), but the commit is unmatched. Same root cause.

## The fix I recommend

**Write the `Reason`-tagged `PRC = 1` on the binding analyzer's own ballot entry, and let the (b)
merge carry it onto the anchor as a consequence.** One write site instead of two. Everything
downstream then works with no further change:

| consumer | why it works |
|---|---|
| eligibility | the existing `PRC <= 0` gates, unchanged — no new predicate at any site |
| ranking | the existing `sortByCostEfficiencyAsc`, unchanged |
| **sizing** | the binder now casts `state[binder][role] / 1`, so `roleBottleneckReplicas` returns a positive `n` and `roleAggRemaining` returns a positive demand — this is the half the anchor-only shape could not reach |
| bound | C11's one-replica ceiling, already landed, unchanged |

The arithmetic closes at one replica: `n = min(bottleneck, 1) = 1` ⇒ `utilByRole = 1·1/demand` ⇒
`deltaUtil = 1/demand` (single role; `min` over roles for P/D, so never larger) ⇒
`k = max(floor(deltaUtil·demand/1), min(1,n)) = 1`. Next iteration headroom is `1-1 = 0` ⇒
`continue` ⇒ `V_run` is picked. Partial scale-from-zero works and the co-tenant is unharmed.

This also makes true a claim the Type 1 **already** asserts at `:1512-1515` — that the sentinel is
"in the binder's own currency, so it is a declared minimum, not a borrowed measurement, and `N8`
stays intact". Anchor-only made that sentence false. Ballot-side makes it correct, so this is a
correction *toward* the frozen design's stated intent, not a new direction.

## Options Dean floated, and why one of them is closed

- **"Use sat's demand."** Structurally unavailable in exactly this configuration. TA binds only when
  saturation fails `Enabled && Live && Informative`; `votingResults` (`:315-323`) prunes the voting
  set on `Enabled && Live`. If saturation failed on `Live` it is not in the voting set at all — no
  donor. If it is Live-but-not-Informative it survives the prune, but *not-Informative means* every
  one of its variant capacities is a no-data/error sentinel, so `prcForVariant <= 0` and it abstains
  anyway. There is no saturation demand to borrow at the moment TA binds. (It would exist in
  `[sat,TA]` with saturation live and informative — but then saturation binds and this question does
  not arise.) `N8` independently rules against borrowing across analyzers on unit-mixing grounds.
- **Turn the `break` into a skip.** Fixes the blast radius but not the feature, and needs a
  per-iteration exclusion set or the re-pick spins. Not a small change; not recommended as the
  quick one.
- **Leave `(D-a)` deferred.** Zero-risk and it is where the coder already left it. Today's behaviour
  is safe; the gap is a missed feature, not a live bug. A legitimate answer to "what ships in PR-2".

## What I am and am not asking for

I am **not** proposing a commit, and I am not asking for PR-2 scope to grow. The disposition is
yours and Dean's. What I am asking is that whichever of these is chosen, it is chosen explicitly —
the thing that must not happen is `(D-a)` landing later, from the frozen Type-1 text, in the
anchor-only shape. That shape ships a regression.

If it stays deferred, the follow-up must carry the ballot-side requirement, not just "write the
sentinel"; otherwise a future implementer reads my Type 1 and builds the broken version.

